## A Surge in Class I Recalls: The Numbers Don't Lie

In 2026, we've already seen 144 Class I recalls, a number that paints a concerning picture of our food safety landscape. This is especially alarming when considering the role of major brands in these incidents. Just recently, multiple recalls were issued due to Salmonella contamination in products containing a specific batch of milk powder.

### The Usual Suspects: Identifying the Repeat Offenders

Among the 82 repeat offenders this year, some familiar names stand out. For example, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company has already faced multiple recalls, including two just last month for Salmonella contamination in their products. Similarly, Solina U.S. Holding has been grappling with a slew of recalls involving cheese powder seasoning.

### A Troubling Trend: Product Categories in the Spotlight

Salmonella contamination has emerged as the leading cause of recalls this year, responsible for 152 incidents. While allergens and foreign objects also account for a significant share, the sheer number of Salmonella outbreaks underscores the need for stringent oversight, especially in high-risk product categories.

### The Immediate Concern: Recent Recalls

The most recent recalls, all reported on May 27, 2026, include products from well-known brands such as:

1. Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, for white chocolate powder and frozen hot cocoa mix.
2. Solina U.S. Holding, for cheddar cheese powder seasoning.
3. PS Seasoning & Spices, Inc., for ranch and parmesan seasonings.

Each of these recalls was driven by the discovery of Salmonella in a common ingredient: milk powder supplied by an external provider. This raises questions about supply chain transparency and the effectiveness of current safety protocols.

### The Bigger Picture: Lessons to Learn

With 858 total recalls on record and a disturbing trend of repeating offenders, it's clear that systemic issues persist in the food industry. While regulatory agencies work to enforce standards, the onus is also on manufacturers to implement rigorous quality controls and traceability measures.

The recent surge in recalls serves as a wake-up call. It's not just about maintaining brand reputation; it's about ensuring public health and safety. Consumers deserve better, and it's high time the industry stepped up to the plate.
